  <h2>Get Up to 10% Back on Your Shopping</h2>
  <p>South Korea charges a 10% VAT (Value Added Tax) on most goods. As a foreign tourist, you're legally entitled to claim this back on qualifying purchases. In Myeongdong, Gangnam, or COEX, a serious shopping trip could yield a refund of ₩50,000–₩300,000 or more. It's free money — don't leave it behind.</p>

  <h2>Who Qualifies?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Non-Korean residents visiting on a tourist visa</li>
    <li>Foreign residents who have been in Korea less than 6 months</li>
    <li>You must take the purchased goods out of Korea unused and unopened (cosmetics: sealed packaging)</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Minimum Purchase Amount</h2>
  <p>Minimum spend at a <strong>single store</strong>: <strong>₩30,000</strong> (approximately $22 USD). No maximum. The more you spend, the bigger the refund.</p>

  <h2>Method 1: Immediate Refund (Best)</h2>
  <p>Many stores in Myeongdong, Dongdaemun, and Insadong offer <strong>즉시환급 (immediate refund)</strong>. You get your tax money back right at the store counter — no airport process needed.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Look for the "즉시환급" or "Tax Free" sticker at the store entrance</li>
    <li>Show your passport at checkout</li>
    <li>Receive the refund in cash or as a discount on your bill</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The refund is slightly less than the full 10% (around 7–8%) due to a small service fee.</p>

  <div class="tip-box">
    💡 <strong>Myeongdong tip:</strong> Almost every K-beauty store, clothing shop, and department store on the main street participates in immediate refund. Always ask if you don't see the sticker.
  </div>

  <h2>Method 2: Airport Refund at Incheon</h2>
  <p>If you shopped at stores without immediate refund, claim at Incheon Airport:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Before check-in:</strong> Go to Customs Stamp Desk. If refund amount exceeds ₩75,000, you must show the goods for inspection.</li>
    <li><strong>After security (departure gates):</strong> Find Global Blue, KTIS, or Easy Tax Refund counters</li>
    <li>Present your passport, receipts, and stamped refund vouchers</li>
    <li>Choose cash (KRW, USD, EUR) or credit card refund</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Method 3: Digital Refund (KTP App)</h2>
  <p>Korea has a growing digital refund system. Some stores are connected to the KTP (Korea Tax-free Platform) — your refund is credited to your credit card automatically within 2–4 weeks. No paper vouchers needed. Ask stores if they support "전자환급 (electronic refund)".</p>

  <h2>Best Shopping Areas for Tax Refunds</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Myeongdong</strong> — Almost universal participation. Immediate refund available everywhere.</li>
    <li><strong>COEX Mall (Gangnam)</strong> — Electronics, luxury, department stores</li>
    <li><strong>Dongdaemun Design Plaza</strong> — Fashion, sportswear, streetwear</li>
    <li><strong>Insadong</strong> — Traditional crafts and souvenirs</li>
    <li><strong>Lotte/Shinsegae Duty Free</strong> — Tax already excluded at point of sale</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Important Rules</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Don't open or use items before leaving Korea (especially cosmetics)</li>
    <li>Keep all receipts and tax refund vouchers organized</li>
    <li>Refund vouchers expire — usually 3 months from purchase date</li>
    <li>Minimum refund for airport processing: ₩30,000 per store, but you can combine across multiple stores</li>
  </ul>

